<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-ga-uniform-b-field" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Vector Potential for a Uniform Magnetic Field</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Note Id:</alert> <tag>202605310001</tag> | <alert>Tags:</alert>
            <tag>geometric algebra</tag> <tag>electromagnetism</tag> <tag>vector potential</tag>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <definition xml:id="def-ga-uniform-b-field-vector-potential">
        <title>Vector Potential</title>
        <idx><term>vector potential</term></idx>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For a uniform magnetic field <m>\vec{B}=B_0\mathbf{e}_z</m>, represented as a bivector <m>\bivec{B}=B_0(\mathbf{e}_x\wedge\mathbf{e}_y)</m>,
                the <term>vector potential</term> <m>\vec{A}</m> must satisfy
                <md>
                    \bivec{B}=\vec{\nabla}\wedge\vec{A} \, .
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
    <title>Common Gauge Choices (Uniform Magnetic Field)</title>
    <dl>

        <li>
            <title><term>Symmetry Gauge</term></title>
            <idx>symmetry gauge</idx>
            <p>
                Preserves rotational symmetry.
                <md>
                    \vec{A}_\text{sym}=\frac{B_0}{2}\left(x\mathbf{e}_x+y\mathbf{e}_y\right)
                </md>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <title><term>Landau Gauge</term></title>
            <idx>landau gauge</idx>
            <p>
                Preserves translational symmetry along the <m>x</m>-axis.
                <md>
                    \vec{A}_\text{landau}=-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x
                </md>
            </p>
        </li>

    </dl>
    </example>

    <lemma>
        <title>Vector Potential for a Uniform Magnetic Field</title>
        <idx>vector potential</idx>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider two gauges of a uniform magnetic field <m>\vec{B}=B_0\mathbf{e}_z</m> with respective potentials <m>\vec{A}_1</m> and <m>\vec{A}_2</m>.
                For such gauges, there is always some scalar function $\chi$ that can be used to relate the respective vector potentials as follows:

                <md>
                  \vec{A}_1= \vec{A}_2 + \vec{\nabla}\chi \, .
                </md>

            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <conclusion>
        🔗 <alert>Linked Notes:</alert> <xref ref="claim-vector-potential-generalization"/>
    </conclusion>

</subsection>
